Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease
Common Infections and Afflictions
Heartburn, Acid regurgitation, Exacerbation when recumbent

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-014
Reason for Visit: Severe retrosternal chest burning and acid regurgitation waking patient from sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing acute, severe retrosternal chest pain radiating to the neck, which awoke them from a recumbent sleep position. While the patient describes a burning sensation, the presence of sudden, severe chest pain radiating to the neck at rest is a red flag for acute coronary syndrome. An immediate on-site clinical assessment is required to evaluate hemodynamic stability, perform a thorough cardiovascular examination, and clinically differentiate a severe esophageal spasm/reflux event from a life-threatening cardiac event. Delaying this assessment poses an extreme risk of missing an acute myocardial infarction, thereby strictly satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I woke up choking on acid, and my chest is burning so intensely I thought I was having a heart attack.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated sharply 2 hours ago after the patient went to bed following a large, late, fatty meal. Patient awoke abruptly with severe heartburn radiating from the epigastrium directly into the neck, accompanied by the regurgitation of sour, acidic gastric contents into the mouth and profound hypersalivation. The patient reports a history of occasional non-cardiac chest pain and a chronic dry cough, particularly at night. Also notes a frequent globus sensation in the throat and mild hoarseness in the morning. Symptoms are heavily exacerbated by lying recumbent. Denies shortness of breath, diaphoresis, left arm radiation, or exertional pain triggers.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently pacing the room in significant distress and refusing to lie down.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High situational anxiety driven by fear of a cardiac event.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert, acutely anxious, frequent throat clearing. No diaphoresis, pallor, or obvious respiratory distress.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Ambulatory 24-hour esophageal pH monitoring demonstrating excessive acid exposure would assist in confirming the diagnosis in refractory outpatient cases but is not acutely indicated.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular rate and rhythm with normal S1/S2 and no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Chest is clear to auscultation bilaterally with equal air entry. Abdominal examination demonstrates mild, diffuse epigastric tenderness to deep palpation without guarding, rigidity, or rebound. Bowel sounds are normal. Oropharynx shows mild posterior erythema consistent with acid irritation.
Environmental Observations: Patient is upright. Remnants of a large, heavy meal are visible in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient self-administered a single liquid antacid 30 minutes ago, which provided temporary, partial alleviation of the burning sensation.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS) - Ruled out clinically by the absence of diaphoresis/pallor, normal hemodynamics, direct correlation with a heavy meal and recumbency, the specific description of acid regurgitation, and the partial relief achieved with simple antacids.
Peptic Ulcer Disease - Unlikely as the primary cause of this acute nighttime presentation, lacking classic features such as pain specifically relieved by food or the presence of melena/hematemesis, though overlap pathology is possible.
Esophageal Spasm - Less likely given the definitive presence of voluminous acid regurgitation and the explicit postural trigger (recumbency), which strongly points to lower esophageal sphincter incompetence.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of severe resting retrosternal chest pain radiating to the neck mandated an urgent clinical assessment to definitively rule out acute coronary syndrome. On-site evaluation confirmed stable hemodynamics, an absence of cardiac ischemic signs, and a classic history of severe gastroesophageal reflux disease exacerbated by recumbency and dietary indiscretion. The provision of high-dose proton pump inhibitors and liquid antacids provided immediate clinical stabilization and reassurance, successfully averting an unnecessary emergency department transport for suspected cardiac ischemia. This critical diagnostic differentiation strictly fulfills the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS 585-600 assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered 20ml oral mucosal protectant/antacid liquid (e.g., Mylanta) stat with excellent immediate symptomatic relief. Prescribed Pantoprazole 40mg daily, to be taken exactly 30 minutes before breakfast for 4 weeks to heal esophageal inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent stomach acid from physically travelling up the esophagus at night, place a 15cm foam wedge under your mattress to elevate your entire torso. Gravity acts as a continuous, physical barrier; elevating the head alone simply bends the neck, whereas elevating the torso keeps the lower esophageal sphincter definitively above the stomach pool.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Foam bed wedge recommended.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 4 weeks to assess response to the Proton Pump Inhibitor and discuss potential eradication testing for H. pylori.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you develop a heavy, crushing sensation in the chest, pain radiating to the left arm or jaw, shortness of breath, severe dizziness, or if you begin sweating profusely.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Chest pain is treated as cardiac until proven otherwise; while today's assessment strongly indicates reflux, symptoms can occasionally overlap.
Clinical Course: The acute burning will subside with strict upright posture and antacids. The chronic hoarseness and nocturnal cough will take 2-4 weeks of daily Pantoprazole therapy to fully resolve as the esophageal lining heals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you vomit material that looks like coffee grounds, or if your stool becomes black and tarry, as these are signs of internal bleeding.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: A physical valve between your stomach and esophagus failed due to the volume of the late meal and lying flat. The prescribed medication (Pantoprazole) stops the stomach from producing acid, rendering the refluxed fluid harmless so the chemical burns in your throat can heal.
Actionable Steps: Strictly avoid eating or drinking anything except water within 3 hours of going to bed. Avoid known chemical relaxants of the esophageal sphincter, including peppermint, chocolate, caffeine, and alcohol.

SMS INFORMATION
